1. Cloudflare can replace Vercel for this lane

For the NayaPOWER applications, I agree with your direction.

We don't need Vercel merely because it's a popular deployment platform.

Cloudflare Workers now deploy Worker code and static assets together as one unit, and Cloudflare's native Git integration can connect a GitHub repository directly to a Worker and deploy on pushes.

So our application stack can simply be:

GitHub
   │
   │ canonical source
   ▼
Cloudflare Workers
   │
   ├── Naya Power Player
   ├── MAXIS
   ├── Naya Activation
   ├── Intelligent Hub
   ├── Communication Hub
   └── future Naya applications

And GitHub Actions does not need to come back into the picture.

Cloudflare's current Workers Builds system performs the build/deploy operation itself; production defaults to npx wrangler deploy, while non-production branches can get preview versions.

So yes:

GitHub governs. Cloudflare runs.

That's a very clean separation.

2. But I would NOT make the user's Superbrain a fork

This is the architectural correction I think matters most.

Your first idea was:

Naya Superbrain
       ↓
     Fork
       ↓
User's Superbrain
       ↓
Intelligent Hub

A GitHub fork is useful for getting someone their own copy of the software/repository.

It is not the right mechanism for collective intelligence synchronization.

Why?

Because a fork is fundamentally a repository relationship.

If someone forks a public repository, they get their own repository in GitHub's repository network. Private forks have additional permission inheritance and policy implications.

But your goal isn't:

"Everyone should have a copy of Shawn's memory."

Your goal is:

"Everyone owns their own Superbrain, and voluntarily contributes selected wisdom to the Collective."

Those are completely different things.

And I think the second one is much more powerful.

